  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nNot much change to the forecast over the past 24 hours. In the short \nterm, upper level riding will allow for temperatures to warm closer \nto normal today, with highs in the mid 70s along the coast, 80s for \ninland spots and High Desert, and around 100 for the lower deserts. \nAfternoon humidity will fall to around 10% for areas east of the \nmountains today, with these drier conditions prevailing into Sunday \neven as temperatures cool a degree or two for Sunday afternoon.\n\nMid-level water vapor satellite shows a deepening low off the coast \nof CA which is expected to move inland late this weekend, kicking \nthe aforementioned ridge eastward. With that, a cooling trend sets \nin beginning Sunday, with more significant cooling Monday as the low \nsettles over Central CA. Southwest flow and lower heights will \nencourage a deeper marine layer Monday and Tuesday mornings, with \nlow (10-15% chance) of some localized marine layer drizzle either of \nthose mornings. Clouds will make it further inland, with slow \nclearing at the coastline in the afternoons. Highs on Monday may not \nbreak above the 70s for many locations, with only double digits in \nthe lower deserts. These highs will generally be 5-13 degrees below \nwhere they normally are for the latter half of September. \n\nThe cooler weather won't prevail beyond Tuesday as the low becomes \nabsorbed into the upper level pattern and swings northward thanks to \na massive low sitting off the Canadian coastline. With the \ndeparture, subsidence will start to build over the SoCal waters late \nnext week. Models still disagree on the extent of high pressure and \nwhere it might set up, as well as the propagation of the Canadian \nlow. Due to this, there remains a large spread on forecasted high \ntemperatures for the latter half of next week and into the weekend, \nalthough general consensus is that temperatures will warm at least \nto near or slightly above normal. The forecast may change in the \ncoming days as models become better aligned with the overall \npattern, but quieter weather seems to prevail for now.",
